1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,155
A voice recording goes in on the left.

2
00:00:02,475 --> 00:00:07,226
Five programs, each in its own container,
turn it into a podcast in English

3
00:00:07,226 --> 00:00:10,837
and in French,
without ever sending each other a message.

4
00:00:11,157 --> 00:00:13,547
So how do they cooperate, and who pays?

5
00:00:13,783 --> 00:00:16,221
Since last lecture, four things change:

6
00:00:16,221 --> 00:00:20,034
you write the Dockerfile,
and you build five images, not one.

7
00:00:20,354 --> 00:00:23,183
A bind mount opens a window
into your laptop,

8
00:00:23,183 --> 00:00:27,018
and the containers reach Google Cloud
through a shared bucket

9
00:00:27,018 --> 00:00:28,653
and a service account key.

10
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:32,143
Open the mega-pipeline
README beside this video.

11
00:00:32,463 --> 00:00:36,549
The tracker, top left, splits the README
into numbered steps;

12
00:00:36,549 --> 00:00:39,161
code panels show file and line numbers.

13
00:00:39,481 --> 00:00:41,845
Pause at the yellow your-turn tag;

14
00:00:41,845 --> 00:00:45,391
a gray extra tag marks
a command not in the README.

15
00:00:45,633 --> 00:00:48,833
You'll need Tutorial 0,
for Docker Desktop and uv,

16
00:00:48,833 --> 00:00:51,905
the course key from Canvas,
and your group name.

17
00:00:52,225 --> 00:00:56,364
Every command is also in the study guide,
linked in the description.

18
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:59,666
One honest limit:
we recorded without a course key,

19
00:00:59,666 --> 00:01:01,890
so wherever the code talks to Google,

20
00:01:01,890 --> 00:01:05,496
you'll see the command and what to look for,
not its output.

21
00:01:05,733 --> 00:01:09,892
We recorded on a Debian 12 laptop
with Docker Engine 29,

22
00:01:09,892 --> 00:01:11,749
at the time of recording.

23
00:01:12,069 --> 00:01:17,157
On a Mac or Windows, the prompt differs
and Docker Desktop runs the containers;

24
00:01:17,157 --> 00:01:18,832
the commands are the same.

25
00:01:19,867 --> 00:01:23,110
In production,
services call each other's APIs.

26
00:01:23,430 --> 00:01:25,040
This tutorial simplifies:

27
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:28,326
each component writes its output
to shared storage,

28
00:01:28,326 --> 00:01:30,107
where the next one reads it.

29
00:01:30,350 --> 00:01:35,598
That storage is one Google Cloud Storage
bucket, drawn as a cabinet of drawers.

30
00:01:35,918 --> 00:01:37,218
The first stage, A,

31
00:01:37,218 --> 00:01:41,870
turns recordings from input audios
into transcripts in text prompts.

32
00:01:42,190 --> 00:01:46,094
Then B turns each transcript
into a longer podcast script,

33
00:01:46,094 --> 00:01:47,374
in text paragraphs.

34
00:01:47,617 --> 00:01:49,132
From there the path splits.

35
00:01:49,452 --> 00:01:54,444
C turns the script into English speech,
stored in the drawer named text audios.

36
00:01:54,764 --> 00:01:57,938
D translates it into French,
in text translated,

37
00:01:57,938 --> 00:02:00,716
and E speaks the French, in output audios.

38
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:02,603
Nothing triggers anything:

39
00:02:02,603 --> 00:02:05,502
you run each component yourself,
in this order.

40
00:02:05,784 --> 00:02:13,609
A bucket has no real folders:
a name like text_prompts/group-01/input-…

41
00:02:13,609 --> 00:02:17,026
.txt is one single object name.

42
00:02:17,267 --> 00:02:21,021
Here's A's upload code:
prefix, group name, file name,

43
00:02:21,021 --> 00:02:23,454
and only files starting with input.

44
00:02:23,774 --> 00:02:28,766
B downloads with the same pattern:
that naming contract is the whole wiring.

45
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,007
The group name keeps teams apart,
and its default has no guard:

46
00:02:33,007 --> 00:02:37,460
a component you forgot to edit writes
into the default group's drawer,

47
00:02:37,460 --> 00:02:39,432
maybe over someone else's work.

48
00:02:39,752 --> 00:02:41,288
Set it before every run.

49
00:02:41,534 --> 00:02:45,310
Each stage that succeeds lights up your row
on a live leaderboard.

50
00:02:45,630 --> 00:02:50,088
Every call and every byte is billed
to the course's project, not yours.

51
00:02:50,334 --> 00:02:54,387
Which two stages could run at the same time,
and which one has to wait?

52
00:03:03,234 --> 00:03:08,482
C and D both read only B's text paragraphs,
so they could run side by side.

53
00:03:08,802 --> 00:03:11,554
E reads what D writes, so it waits for D.

54
00:03:12,600 --> 00:03:15,416
The README's first command: clone the repo.

55
00:03:15,736 --> 00:03:20,237
Type git clone and the address,
copied from the README, and pause to run it.

56
00:03:20,483 --> 00:03:22,254
Then cd mega-pipeline.

57
00:03:22,574 --> 00:03:27,350
An extra check, not in the README,
lists the files and shows the commit:

58
00:03:27,350 --> 00:03:32,259
five component folders, on the main branch,
at the commit this video uses.

59
00:03:32,500 --> 00:03:34,889
Another extra lists all five folders.

60
00:03:35,209 --> 00:03:39,289
Each has a README, its own cli.py,
and a secrets folder,

61
00:03:39,289 --> 00:03:44,681
and not one has a Dockerfile:
the README says you write all five yourself.

62
00:03:44,917 --> 00:03:46,560
Two differences to remember.

63
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:51,779
The generate text folder already
has its own pyproject.toml

64
00:03:51,779 --> 00:03:53,356
and a main.py file,

65
00:03:53,356 --> 00:03:55,242
but no .gitignore file.

66
00:03:55,562 --> 00:03:58,037
And synthesis audio has a bonus script.

67
00:03:58,283 --> 00:03:59,862
Now the first component.

68
00:04:00,182 --> 00:04:03,430
Open the README
in the transcribe audio folder:

69
00:04:03,430 --> 00:04:07,990
the tracker switches to it,
and its step 1 is cd transcribe_audio.

70
00:04:08,310 --> 00:04:10,017
Pause, and catch up to here.

71
00:04:10,267 --> 00:04:12,614
Open cli.py in your editor.

72
00:04:12,934 --> 00:04:15,297
The first imports come with Python,

73
00:04:15,297 --> 00:04:18,672
but the two Google Cloud modules
and ffmpeg don't.

74
00:04:18,992 --> 00:04:20,891
Part 2 shows where they come from.

75
00:04:21,133 --> 00:04:22,716
Next come four settings:

76
00:04:22,716 --> 00:04:26,081
the bucket's name,
the two drawers this stage uses,

77
00:04:26,081 --> 00:04:28,060
and on line 21 the group name,

78
00:04:28,060 --> 00:04:31,160
still at its default,
which you'll change soon.

79
00:04:31,400 --> 00:04:34,260
At the bottom, main runs up to three verbs,

80
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:37,651
always in this order:
download, transcribe, upload.

81
00:04:39,633 --> 00:04:41,139
Step 2 is the key:

82
00:04:41,139 --> 00:04:44,902
a file from Canvas
called mega-pipeline.json.

83
00:04:45,222 --> 00:04:48,689
It lets your code act
as the course's service account,

84
00:04:48,689 --> 00:04:52,220
a robot Google account,
so guard it like a credit card.

85
00:04:52,466 --> 00:04:56,299
Pause, download it,
and put it in this folder's secrets folder,

86
00:04:56,299 --> 00:04:58,610
with a copy in each of the other four.

87
00:04:59,716 --> 00:05:01,231
The name must be exact.

88
00:05:01,551 --> 00:05:05,306
If your browser saved it with a 1
in parentheses, rename it.

89
00:05:05,866 --> 00:05:06,945
A small mistake:

90
00:05:06,945 --> 00:05:10,381
the component READMEs link
to an older Canvas file,

91
00:05:10,381 --> 00:05:12,373
so use the main README's link.

92
00:05:12,616 --> 00:05:14,323
Step 3 uses uv.

93
00:05:14,643 --> 00:05:18,753
After Tutorial 0,
uv --version prints a version,

94
00:05:18,753 --> 00:05:20,893
so skip ahead to uv init.

95
00:05:21,213 --> 00:05:24,840
This recording laptop had no uv:
command not found.

96
00:05:25,083 --> 00:05:30,065
Otherwise, use the README's installer:
on macOS or Linux,

97
00:05:30,065 --> 00:05:31,396
this curl line,

98
00:05:31,396 --> 00:05:35,920
which installed uv 0.13.0
at the time of recording.

99
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:40,528
On Windows, the PowerShell line;
on any system, pip install uv.

100
00:05:40,766 --> 00:05:44,266
Then restart your terminal,
as the installer says,

101
00:05:44,266 --> 00:05:47,486
cd back into transcribe audio,
and uv answers.

102
00:05:47,933 --> 00:05:51,261
Now the step itself: uv init --bare.

103
00:05:51,581 --> 00:05:57,896
It writes just one file, pyproject.toml,
for a project named transcribe-audio.

104
00:05:58,216 --> 00:05:59,368
Pause and run it.

105
00:05:59,616 --> 00:06:01,304
An extra look inside:

106
00:06:01,304 --> 00:06:05,240
a name,
a version, an empty list of dependencies,

107
00:06:05,240 --> 00:06:08,171
and the minimum Python version, 3.11.

108
00:06:08,416 --> 00:06:13,600
That 3.11 comes from this laptop's Python,
3.11.2.

109
00:06:13,920 --> 00:06:17,748
In the image,
uv sync needs a Python in this range,

110
00:06:17,748 --> 00:06:20,299
and 3.12 is in it, so all is well.

111
00:06:20,533 --> 00:06:22,666
Your number depends on your laptop.

112
00:06:22,986 --> 00:06:25,708
If yours says 3.13 or newer,

113
00:06:25,708 --> 00:06:30,666
the image's 3.12 falls outside,
so edit it to 3.12.

114
00:06:30,986 --> 00:06:34,549
That fix isn't in the README;
generate text shows why.

115
00:06:35,883 --> 00:06:38,107
Step 4 is the heart of this lecture:

116
00:06:38,107 --> 00:06:42,368
there's no Dockerfile here,
so you write it yourself, piece by piece.

117
00:06:42,616 --> 00:06:47,885
Line 1 starts from the official
Python 3.12 image on Debian 12.

118
00:06:48,205 --> 00:06:51,384
The README names it in its text,
not in a code block.

119
00:06:51,633 --> 00:06:57,492
Line 3 declares a build argument, a variable
that exists only during the build:

120
00:06:57,492 --> 00:07:00,977
the Debian packages
build-essential and ffmpeg.

121
00:07:01,297 --> 00:07:07,036
The Python package
ffmpeg-python just runs the ffmpeg program.

122
00:07:07,283 --> 00:07:10,846
Lines 5 to 10 set six environment variables.

123
00:07:11,166 --> 00:07:12,306
As in lecture 2,

124
00:07:12,306 --> 00:07:15,582
they only write on the config card:
no layers.

125
00:07:15,902 --> 00:07:19,980
The first four:
apt never asks questions, text is Unicode,

126
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:24,691
Python prints at once,
and uv copies files instead of linking them.

127
00:07:24,933 --> 00:07:26,928
Line 9 matters most:

128
00:07:26,928 --> 00:07:33,210
it tells uv to build the venv
in /home/app/.venv, outside /app.

129
00:07:33,530 --> 00:07:35,152
Soon, this line saves us.

130
00:07:35,400 --> 00:07:39,724
Line 10 tells Google's libraries
where the key is:

131
00:07:39,724 --> 00:07:43,528
secrets/mega-pipeline.json, a relative path.

132
00:07:43,766 --> 00:07:47,115
Lines 12 to 21 are one RUN instruction.

133
00:07:47,435 --> 00:07:51,864
Every line but the last ends in a backslash,
which continues it,

134
00:07:51,864 --> 00:07:55,393
and && chains the commands,
so they make one layer.

135
00:07:55,713 --> 00:07:58,315
It updates Debian, installs our packages,

136
00:07:58,315 --> 00:08:01,723
and deletes the apt package lists
in that same layer,

137
00:08:01,723 --> 00:08:03,073
so they cost nothing.

138
00:08:03,316 --> 00:08:06,153
Then it upgrades pip and installs
uv with it.

139
00:08:06,473 --> 00:08:09,385
Unlike Debian's system Python in lecture 1,

140
00:08:09,385 --> 00:08:13,514
this Python belongs to the image,
so pip may install into it.

141
00:08:13,750 --> 00:08:18,948
Last, it creates a user called app,
user ID 1000, who owns /app:

142
00:08:18,948 --> 00:08:23,414
as the README says,
running as root is a security risk.

143
00:08:23,650 --> 00:08:29,154
Lines 23 and 24 switch to that user and make
/app the working folder.

144
00:08:29,474 --> 00:08:33,367
Line 26 copies everything
in this folder into the image;

145
00:08:33,367 --> 00:08:35,660
hold on to that word, everything.

146
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:40,992
Line 27 runs uv sync
to build the venv from pyproject.toml,

147
00:08:40,992 --> 00:08:47,206
and lines 29 and 30 start bash
with the venv activated, as in lecture 2.

148
00:08:50,733 --> 00:08:54,392
The main README's appendix
has a sample Dockerfile,

149
00:08:54,392 --> 00:08:57,978
but its build argument
has curl instead of ffmpeg:

150
00:08:57,978 --> 00:09:00,632
don't paste it into transcribe audio.

151
00:09:00,866 --> 00:09:05,517
Pause now and write it:
a file named Dockerfile, with no extension.

152
00:09:05,837 --> 00:09:07,091
Type the FROM line,

153
00:09:07,091 --> 00:09:10,259
then copy the README's six code
blocks below it,

154
00:09:10,259 --> 00:09:13,185
in order,
with one blank line between blocks,

155
00:09:13,185 --> 00:09:15,266
so your line numbers match ours.

156
00:09:16,050 --> 00:09:19,101
First, an extra check: your Dockerfile,

157
00:09:19,101 --> 00:09:22,855
named exactly that,
sits next to pyproject.toml.

158
00:09:23,100 --> 00:09:24,977
Step 5 builds the image.

159
00:09:25,297 --> 00:09:30,033
A small gap: the README names it,
transcribe audio, but gives no command.

160
00:09:30,353 --> 00:09:33,191
Here it is:
docker build -t transcribe_audio,

161
00:09:33,191 --> 00:09:36,092
then a dot for this folder,
the build context.

162
00:09:36,333 --> 00:09:37,485
Pause and run it.

163
00:09:37,805 --> 00:09:40,152
Your terminal shows a compact live view

164
00:09:40,152 --> 00:09:43,522
that folds each build step into one line
when it's done.

165
00:09:43,842 --> 00:09:49,794
Here are the full log's landmarks;
to see them yourself, add --progress=plain.

166
00:09:50,114 --> 00:09:55,981
Build step 2 is our big RUN:
apt installs 237 packages,

167
00:09:55,981 --> 00:10:03,128
a 205 megabyte download,
then pip installs uv, all in 46.9 seconds.

168
00:10:03,367 --> 00:10:07,442
Build steps 3 and 4 set the working folder
and copy our files.

169
00:10:07,762 --> 00:10:12,609
Build step 5 runs uv sync
with the image's own Python 3.12,

170
00:10:12,609 --> 00:10:14,006
creates the venv,

171
00:10:14,006 --> 00:10:19,601
and resolves 1 package: the project itself,
with no dependencies yet.

172
00:10:19,850 --> 00:10:23,800
It ends by naming the image,
with a warning about line 10:

173
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:27,274
Docker flags any variable
whose name sounds secret.

174
00:10:27,594 --> 00:10:31,818
The value is just a path, so it's harmless,
and you'll see it every build.

175
00:10:32,067 --> 00:10:33,313
An extra check,

176
00:10:33,313 --> 00:10:37,550
docker images,
says 1.35 gigabytes on this machine.

177
00:10:37,870 --> 00:10:43,222
By far the biggest layer is that one RUN,
842 megabytes of compilers,

178
00:10:43,222 --> 00:10:47,875
ffmpeg and their libraries,
while the copied folder is tiny.

179
00:10:48,117 --> 00:10:51,609
And if you skipped uv init,
build step 5 fails,

180
00:10:51,609 --> 00:10:54,730
because there's no pyproject.toml to sync.

181
00:10:54,967 --> 00:10:55,967
Ponder this.

182
00:10:56,268 --> 00:11:00,430
You change only the group name in cli.py
and build again:

183
00:11:00,430 --> 00:11:03,351
which of the five build steps run again?

184
00:11:12,200 --> 00:11:13,821
Build steps 4 and 5.

185
00:11:14,141 --> 00:11:17,458
The copied folder changed,
so the copy runs again,

186
00:11:17,458 --> 00:11:19,581
and so does every step after it.

187
00:11:19,901 --> 00:11:22,591
Build steps 2 and 3 come from the cache,

188
00:11:22,591 --> 00:11:26,088
and build step 1
is the base image you already have.

189
00:11:26,408 --> 00:11:28,008
We'll see it in part 2.

190
00:11:29,650 --> 00:11:32,743
Step 6 runs the image, so first, a picture.

191
00:11:33,063 --> 00:11:37,842
The copied files sit in /app;
the venv sits outside it, in /home.

192
00:11:38,162 --> 00:11:42,941
As in lecture 2,
a container adds a thin writable layer,

193
00:11:42,941 --> 00:11:45,330
thrown away on exit by --rm.

194
00:11:45,567 --> 00:11:49,445
-v mounts the current folder,
pwd, onto /app:

195
00:11:49,445 --> 00:11:54,185
it covers the copied files,
so /app is now your folder.

196
00:11:54,433 --> 00:11:57,612
So edits in VS Code show up inside at once.

197
00:11:57,932 --> 00:12:02,773
Anything written under /app goes straight
into your laptop's folder and stays;

198
00:12:02,773 --> 00:12:05,441
anything else lands on the throwaway layer.

199
00:12:05,683 --> 00:12:06,683
Ponder this.

200
00:12:06,984 --> 00:12:09,843
In lecture 2, the venv sat next to the code.

201
00:12:10,163 --> 00:12:11,465
If it sat in /app here,

202
00:12:11,465 --> 00:12:14,409
what would the container
find there with this mount?

203
00:12:23,250 --> 00:12:25,331
It would find your laptop's folder,

204
00:12:25,331 --> 00:12:28,007
with no venv in it,
so activation would fail.

205
00:12:28,327 --> 00:12:32,082
That's what line 9,
UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT, prevents.

206
00:12:32,317 --> 00:12:38,034
Type docker run, --rm, -ti,
-v with the mount, and the image name.

207
00:12:38,354 --> 00:12:42,066
Copy it from the README, quotes included,
and pause to run it.

208
00:12:42,300 --> 00:12:48,233
On Windows, run these commands in Git BASH,
keep the quotes around pwd,

209
00:12:48,233 --> 00:12:50,097
and if docker run says

210
00:12:50,097 --> 00:12:54,417
the input device is not a TTY,
put winpty in front.

211
00:12:54,667 --> 00:12:57,943
The prompt changes:
you're user app, in /app,

212
00:12:57,943 --> 00:13:01,346
and after the @
sign comes the container's ID,

213
00:13:01,346 --> 00:13:02,603
new on every run.

214
00:13:02,850 --> 00:13:07,357
No venv name shows in the prompt,
yet the venv is active:

215
00:13:07,357 --> 00:13:11,469
two extra checks find python in /home,
version 3.12.

216
00:13:11,717 --> 00:13:12,781
Two more extras:

217
00:13:12,781 --> 00:13:15,775
listing the folder
shows your laptop's files,

218
00:13:15,775 --> 00:13:18,458
and the key variable from line 10 is set.

219
00:13:18,700 --> 00:13:22,028
Last extra: ask cli.py for its help.

220
00:13:22,348 --> 00:13:27,831
ModuleNotFoundError, No module named google:
the packages aren't installed yet.

221
00:13:28,067 --> 00:13:32,248
The container runs and sees your folder,
but Python can't find Google.

222
00:13:32,568 --> 00:13:35,453
If you stop here, rerun the same docker run

223
00:13:35,453 --> 00:13:38,606
from the transcribe audio
folder before part 2.

224
00:13:38,926 --> 00:13:42,872
In part 2, we add the packages,
and find out why they vanish.

225
00:13:44,517 --> 00:13:45,562
Welcome back.

226
00:13:45,882 --> 00:13:48,645
We're inside the transcribe audio container,

227
00:13:48,645 --> 00:13:51,408
and Python can't find Google's packages yet.

228
00:13:51,650 --> 00:13:55,118
Step 7:
the README lists three packages to add,

229
00:13:55,118 --> 00:13:58,071
so type all three in one uv add command.

230
00:13:58,391 --> 00:13:59,544
Pause and run it.

231
00:14:01,684 --> 00:14:04,863
Three packages asked for, and 26 installed.

232
00:14:05,183 --> 00:14:09,311
As in lecture 1,
each package brings its own dependencies,

233
00:14:09,311 --> 00:14:11,518
and uv resolves the whole tree.

234
00:14:11,767 --> 00:14:13,388
But where did everything land?

235
00:14:13,708 --> 00:14:18,738
The list, pyproject.toml,
and the receipt, uv.lock, sit in /app,

236
00:14:18,738 --> 00:14:21,410
so they're written to your laptop.

237
00:14:21,730 --> 00:14:27,319
The 26 packages went into the venv in /home,
on the container's throwaway layer.

238
00:14:27,567 --> 00:14:28,890
Two extra checks.

239
00:14:29,210 --> 00:14:33,464
The pyproject.toml now
lists the three packages,

240
00:14:33,464 --> 00:14:35,592
each with a lower bound,

241
00:14:35,592 --> 00:14:42,586
and a new uv.lock of about 138 kilobytes
sits next to cli.py, owned by user app.

242
00:14:42,834 --> 00:14:47,615
Another extra:
the venv variable says /home/app/.venv,

243
00:14:47,615 --> 00:14:49,298
outside the window.

244
00:14:49,534 --> 00:14:50,643
Two asides.

245
00:14:50,963 --> 00:14:56,361
The package is ffmpeg-python;
plain ffmpeg is another project.

246
00:14:56,681 --> 00:15:01,546
And on a Linux laptop
whose folder isn't owned by user ID 1000,

247
00:15:01,546 --> 00:15:07,262
uv add fails with permission denied:
the chown command on screen fixes it.

248
00:15:09,217 --> 00:15:12,332
The README adds:
when you exit, rebuild the image.

249
00:15:12,652 --> 00:15:14,785
We'll do that at the end, and see why.

250
00:15:15,034 --> 00:15:16,869
Step 8: set your group name.

251
00:15:17,189 --> 00:15:24,509
On your laptop, in VS Code, open cli.py
and change line 21 to your team's name;

252
00:15:24,509 --> 00:15:26,085
ours is group-01.

253
00:15:26,405 --> 00:15:27,962
Pause and make the change.

254
00:15:28,200 --> 00:15:31,390
Inside,
an extra grep already shows the new line:

255
00:15:31,390 --> 00:15:33,149
the window works both ways.

256
00:15:33,469 --> 00:15:40,040
Use the same name in all five cli.py files,
or the stages won't find each other's work.

257
00:15:40,284 --> 00:15:43,932
Step 9: python cli.py --help.

258
00:15:44,252 --> 00:15:45,335
This time it works,

259
00:15:45,335 --> 00:15:48,071
and it matches the README's block line
for line.

260
00:15:48,391 --> 00:15:50,118
Pause, and get to this point.

261
00:15:51,967 --> 00:15:59,348
Step 10 downloads the recordings:
python cli.py --download, or -d for short.

262
00:15:59,668 --> 00:16:03,359
Our machine had no key,
so here's what a missing key looks like.

263
00:16:03,600 --> 00:16:07,291
It printed the arguments
and the word download, then a traceback.

264
00:16:07,611 --> 00:16:11,250
The last line says DefaultCredentialsError:

265
00:16:11,250 --> 00:16:15,483
the file secrets/mega-pipeline.json
was not found.

266
00:16:15,717 --> 00:16:17,445
Read it as in lecture 1.

267
00:16:17,765 --> 00:16:20,858
Our code, line 36, built a storage client.

268
00:16:21,178 --> 00:16:24,530
The client asked
Google's auth library for credentials,

269
00:16:24,530 --> 00:16:27,152
and that library looked for a missing file.

270
00:16:27,400 --> 00:16:29,064
Here's the chain when it works.

271
00:16:29,384 --> 00:16:31,393
Your code never passes the key:

272
00:16:31,393 --> 00:16:34,504
the client asks Google's auth library
who it is.

273
00:16:34,824 --> 00:16:38,699
That search is called
application default credentials,

274
00:16:38,699 --> 00:16:42,718
or ADC, and its first stop
is the variable from line 10.

275
00:16:42,967 --> 00:16:45,368
That variable is a signpost:

276
00:16:45,368 --> 00:16:51,116
secrets/mega-pipeline.json,
starting from the current folder, /app.

277
00:16:51,436 --> 00:16:54,444
Through the window,
it leads to the key on your laptop.

278
00:16:54,834 --> 00:16:58,558
From the key, the library makes
a short-lived access token,

279
00:16:58,558 --> 00:17:01,020
and every request to Google carries it.

280
00:17:01,267 --> 00:17:04,104
Google then checks twice,
as the README says.

281
00:17:04,424 --> 00:17:06,809
Authentication asks who is calling,

282
00:17:06,809 --> 00:17:10,286
and the key says it's
the course's service account;

283
00:17:10,286 --> 00:17:15,262
authorization asks whether it may do this,
and IAM roles open the bucket.

284
00:17:15,500 --> 00:17:17,676
This error has three usual causes.

285
00:17:17,996 --> 00:17:21,261
The key isn't in this
component's secrets folder,

286
00:17:21,261 --> 00:17:23,728
you changed directory away from /app,

287
00:17:23,728 --> 00:17:27,127
or you ran docker run
from the wrong laptop folder.

288
00:17:31,067 --> 00:17:32,175
One extra check:

289
00:17:32,175 --> 00:17:37,022
even this failed run created two folders
on your laptop, input audios,

290
00:17:37,022 --> 00:17:39,792
and text prompts with your group inside.

291
00:17:40,112 --> 00:17:42,736
The code makes them
before it builds the client.

292
00:17:43,784 --> 00:17:48,630
With your key, look for the word download,
one object name per recording,

293
00:17:48,630 --> 00:17:52,680
and the audio files appearing
in input audios on your laptop.

294
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,837
Pause, check your key
is in secrets, and run it.

295
00:17:58,383 --> 00:18:00,132
Step 11 transcribes.

296
00:18:00,452 --> 00:18:05,118
For each recording,
ffmpeg first converts the MP3 into FLAC,

297
00:18:05,118 --> 00:18:08,772
and that's why ffmpeg
is in the build argument.

298
00:18:09,016 --> 00:18:11,597
The audio bytes travel inside the request.

299
00:18:11,917 --> 00:18:15,898
The long-running recognize call
hands back an operation at once,

300
00:18:15,898 --> 00:18:17,080
like a ticket stub.

301
00:18:17,400 --> 00:18:21,326
Its result call then waits up
to 90 seconds for the transcript.

302
00:18:21,833 --> 00:18:27,378
Long audio comes back in pieces,
so lines 84 to 91 join every piece;

303
00:18:27,378 --> 00:18:30,068
that was the latest commit's fix.

304
00:18:30,316 --> 00:18:34,753
Type python cli.py --transcribe, or -t.

305
00:18:35,073 --> 00:18:37,687
Look for Transcribing and a file name,

306
00:18:37,687 --> 00:18:40,919
ffmpeg's log, the response,
and the transcript.

307
00:18:41,239 --> 00:18:43,338
If it says no such file,

308
00:18:43,338 --> 00:18:47,447
ffmpeg,
add ffmpeg to line 3, exit and rebuild.

309
00:18:47,683 --> 00:18:51,804
Then open the new text file
under text prompts, group-01,

310
00:18:51,804 --> 00:18:52,888
on your laptop.

311
00:18:53,208 --> 00:18:56,182
If it says None,
Speech-to-Text returned nothing:

312
00:18:56,182 --> 00:19:00,248
delete it and transcribe again,
because finished files are skipped.

313
00:19:00,483 --> 00:19:05,368
Step 12: python cli.py --upload, or -u.

314
00:19:05,688 --> 00:19:10,535
It uploads only files starting with input,
under the same name in the bucket:

315
00:19:10,535 --> 00:19:14,563
look for Uploading, then text prompts,
your group, and the file.

316
00:19:14,800 --> 00:19:17,474
Your transcript drops
into your group's drawer,

317
00:19:17,474 --> 00:19:20,489
and the transcribe cell lights up
on the leaderboard,

318
00:19:20,489 --> 00:19:22,651
which is how the README says to check.

319
00:19:22,971 --> 00:19:25,616
Pause and run all three steps,
with your key.

320
00:19:25,850 --> 00:19:32,058
An extra tip: the flags combine,
so -d -t -u runs all three, in order.

321
00:19:32,378 --> 00:19:34,660
Each call is billed to the course project.

322
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,453
Step 13: exit, and rebuild.

323
00:19:38,773 --> 00:19:43,121
Type exit, and the container
is removed with its throwaway layer,

324
00:19:43,121 --> 00:19:45,195
and the 26 packages go with it.

325
00:19:45,433 --> 00:19:50,695
Back on the laptop, an extra check:
the pyproject.toml with three dependencies,

326
00:19:50,695 --> 00:19:54,199
the lock file,
and the two new folders all survived,

327
00:19:54,199 --> 00:19:56,356
because they were in the window.

328
00:19:56,600 --> 00:19:59,928
Git status shows only cli.py as changed.

329
00:20:00,248 --> 00:20:02,979
We'll see why the rest
is invisible at the end.

330
00:20:03,217 --> 00:20:04,924
Now the trap, as an extra.

331
00:20:05,244 --> 00:20:08,448
Start a new container
from the image you built earlier,

332
00:20:08,448 --> 00:20:09,788
and ask for help again.

333
00:20:10,108 --> 00:20:12,013
It's ModuleNotFoundError again:

334
00:20:12,013 --> 00:20:14,779
the packages lived only
in the old container.

335
00:20:15,017 --> 00:20:16,150
The README's fix:

336
00:20:16,150 --> 00:20:20,350
exit, and rebuild the image
with the same docker build command.

337
00:20:20,670 --> 00:20:21,822
Pause and run it.

338
00:20:22,067 --> 00:20:26,528
The ponder's answer, for real:
build steps 2 and 3 are cached,

339
00:20:26,528 --> 00:20:31,043
while build step 4 copies
the changed folder and build step 5,

340
00:20:31,043 --> 00:20:32,499
uv sync, runs again.

341
00:20:32,819 --> 00:20:34,589
The build took under ten seconds.

342
00:20:34,833 --> 00:20:38,172
The full log shows
what uv sync did this time:

343
00:20:38,172 --> 00:20:40,641
the copy brought in the lock file,

344
00:20:40,641 --> 00:20:44,561
and uv installed the same 26 packages,
into the image.

345
00:20:44,800 --> 00:20:46,016
Two more extras.

346
00:20:46,336 --> 00:20:48,973
The image grew to 1.48 gigabytes,

347
00:20:48,973 --> 00:20:52,888
and docker history
lists one row per instruction:

348
00:20:52,888 --> 00:20:55,445
card-only lines show zero bytes.

349
00:20:55,765 --> 00:21:02,272
The uv sync layer now holds 105 megabytes,
the packages plus uv's download cache.

350
00:21:02,517 --> 00:21:07,699
And a fresh container now imports
all three packages with no uv add at all:

351
00:21:07,699 --> 00:21:08,874
they're baked in.

352
00:21:09,500 --> 00:21:10,591
One more thing:

353
00:21:10,591 --> 00:21:14,082
because the copy comes before uv sync,
any edit,

354
00:21:14,082 --> 00:21:18,951
even the group name, knocks over uv sync,
like lecture 2's dominoes.

355
00:21:19,271 --> 00:21:24,177
Later tutorials copy pyproject.toml
and uv.lock first.

356
00:21:25,617 --> 00:21:29,401
Component B,
generate text, follows the same recipe;

357
00:21:29,401 --> 00:21:30,566
we'll go faster.

358
00:21:30,886 --> 00:21:36,326
Steps 1 and 2: cd into its folder,
and copy the key into its secrets folder.

359
00:21:36,567 --> 00:21:41,691
An extra look: this folder already
has its own pyproject.toml,

360
00:21:41,691 --> 00:21:46,402
listing both packages
and requiring Python 3.14 or newer.

361
00:21:46,722 --> 00:21:48,642
Leave that until step 13.

362
00:21:48,884 --> 00:21:53,620
Surprise one: uv init fails:
the project is already initialized.

363
00:21:53,940 --> 00:21:57,620
The README still says to run it,
a small mistake:

364
00:21:57,620 --> 00:22:01,001
skip step 3,
and ignore the leftover main.py.

365
00:22:01,250 --> 00:22:05,585
Step 4: the same Dockerfile,
except line 3 drops ffmpeg,

366
00:22:05,585 --> 00:22:08,759
since this component never touches audio.

367
00:22:09,079 --> 00:22:11,256
Copy yours and change that line.

368
00:22:11,500 --> 00:22:14,812
Step 5 builds it,
and the big RUN step runs again,

369
00:22:14,812 --> 00:22:17,793
because the package
list it installs changed.

370
00:22:18,113 --> 00:22:19,266
Pause and run it.

371
00:22:19,500 --> 00:22:20,500
Ponder this.

372
00:22:20,801 --> 00:22:28,033
The image has Python 3.12;
this pyproject.toml asks for 3.14 or newer.

373
00:22:28,353 --> 00:22:30,124
Which Python will the venv use?

374
00:22:38,967 --> 00:22:43,686
Surprise two: the live view hides it;
the full log shows it:

375
00:22:43,686 --> 00:22:45,967
uv downloaded its own Python,

376
00:22:45,967 --> 00:22:50,530
3.15.0 at the time of recording,
and built the venv on it.

377
00:22:50,767 --> 00:22:54,116
The image's 3.12 falls outside the interval,

378
00:22:54,116 --> 00:22:58,532
so uv fetched the newest Python in range,
as in lecture 2.

379
00:22:58,767 --> 00:23:03,864
Step 6 runs it,
and an extra check confirms Python 3.15.0,

380
00:23:03,864 --> 00:23:06,852
from uv's folder, not the image's.

381
00:23:07,100 --> 00:23:11,917
Step 7's uv add finds nothing new,
since both packages are listed;

382
00:23:11,917 --> 00:23:14,034
it only writes the lock file.

383
00:23:14,284 --> 00:23:17,639
Steps 8 and 9:
set the group name, here on line 19,

384
00:23:17,639 --> 00:23:20,599
and check the help,
which matches the README.

385
00:23:20,919 --> 00:23:22,198
Pause and catch up.

386
00:23:22,434 --> 00:23:25,805
Steps 10 to 12: download, generate, upload.

387
00:23:26,125 --> 00:23:32,695
The generate step sends each transcript
to Gemini 2.5 Flash Lite on Vertex AI.

388
00:23:33,015 --> 00:23:37,410
Google retires that model
on October 20, 2026.

389
00:23:37,730 --> 00:23:40,880
After that,
start the container with a current model

390
00:23:40,880 --> 00:23:42,637
in the GEMINI_MODEL variable.

391
00:23:42,957 --> 00:23:47,354
Newer models aren't served
in the region cli.py uses,

392
00:23:47,354 --> 00:23:51,170
so also set the location
on line 24 to global.

393
00:23:52,017 --> 00:23:56,988
Look for your prompt, Generated text
and a long podcast script, then the upload.

394
00:23:57,308 --> 00:23:58,865
Pause and run all three.

395
00:23:59,900 --> 00:24:06,812
Step 13, exit and rebuild, brings the fix:
lower the minimum Python version to 3.12.

396
00:24:07,132 --> 00:24:11,868
Git status lists the Dockerfile too:
this folder has no .gitignore.

397
00:24:13,817 --> 00:24:15,335
Pause and rebuild:

398
00:24:15,335 --> 00:24:21,156
uv sync now uses the image's Python 3.12,
as does the next container.

399
00:24:21,476 --> 00:24:22,476
Exit it.

400
00:24:22,734 --> 00:24:25,444
Better still,
fix this before your first build.

401
00:24:26,783 --> 00:24:28,916
Speed round: C, D and E.

402
00:24:29,236 --> 00:24:33,247
For C, synthesis_audio_en,
cd in, copy the key,

403
00:24:33,247 --> 00:24:34,612
and run uv init.

404
00:24:34,932 --> 00:24:40,756
If it stamps 3.13 or newer,
change it to 3.12, here and in D and E.

405
00:24:41,000 --> 00:24:43,176
Write the same Dockerfile as B's.

406
00:24:43,496 --> 00:24:48,065
A small slip, here and in E:
the README's text says to keep ffmpeg,

407
00:24:48,065 --> 00:24:51,474
but its code block,
which is right, leaves it out.

408
00:24:51,716 --> 00:24:56,692
Build it: build step 2 is cached
and the build takes 0.6 seconds:

409
00:24:56,692 --> 00:25:02,127
C's Dockerfile matches B's up to there,
so Docker reuses B's big layer.

410
00:25:02,366 --> 00:25:06,718
Run it, and add storage and text to speech:
24 packages.

411
00:25:06,966 --> 00:25:10,102
Set the group name on line 18,
then check the help.

412
00:25:10,422 --> 00:25:12,517
Without a key, even help fails:

413
00:25:12,517 --> 00:25:17,245
line 22 builds the Text-to-Speech client
as soon as the script starts,

414
00:25:17,245 --> 00:25:18,934
before reading any flags.

415
00:25:19,254 --> 00:25:20,534
Pause and catch up.

416
00:25:22,250 --> 00:25:26,666
The request carries the text
and an address in the bucket, line 78.

417
00:25:26,986 --> 00:25:30,413
Google's long audio service
writes the speech straight there,

418
00:25:30,413 --> 00:25:32,042
never through your container.

419
00:25:32,283 --> 00:25:35,874
C just holds a ticket stub
for up to 300 seconds,

420
00:25:35,874 --> 00:25:40,710
then prints that the audio will be saved:
so C has no upload step.

421
00:25:40,950 --> 00:25:44,217
So in C,
pause to run download and synthesis,

422
00:25:44,217 --> 00:25:45,814
then exit and rebuild.

423
00:25:46,800 --> 00:25:50,519
D, translate text, races
through the same setup:

424
00:25:50,519 --> 00:25:54,160
cd, key, uv init,
the same Dockerfile, a build.

425
00:25:54,483 --> 00:25:59,326
Then run it, add storage and translate,
and set the group name on line 18.

426
00:25:59,646 --> 00:26:01,864
Like C, even help needs the key.

427
00:26:02,184 --> 00:26:03,464
Pause and catch up.

428
00:26:06,333 --> 00:26:10,586
Translation has no ticket stub:
the French comes straight back,

429
00:26:10,586 --> 00:26:14,973
is printed and saved under text translated,
so D needs an upload.

430
00:26:15,216 --> 00:26:19,782
Pause to run download, translate and upload,
then exit and rebuild.

431
00:26:21,066 --> 00:26:25,482
E, synthesis audio, is C again,
group name on line 18 too.

432
00:26:25,802 --> 00:26:27,061
One harmless slip:

433
00:26:27,061 --> 00:26:30,627
its help text says
“Transcribe audio file to text”,

434
00:26:30,627 --> 00:26:32,074
pasted from A's code.

435
00:26:32,394 --> 00:26:33,674
Pause and catch up.

436
00:26:36,533 --> 00:26:39,541
Pause, and run download
and synthesis with your key.

437
00:26:40,216 --> 00:26:45,613
An optional bonus in E speaks the French
with ElevenLabs, in the instructor's voice.

438
00:26:45,933 --> 00:26:50,600
It uses your own ElevenLabs key and quota:
a key file in secrets,

439
00:26:50,600 --> 00:26:54,979
your group name in the bonus script too,
and uv add requests.

440
00:26:55,216 --> 00:26:56,837
Then run its three commands.

441
00:26:57,157 --> 00:26:59,766
Without the key file, even download fails:

442
00:26:59,766 --> 00:27:02,747
the script reads that file
as soon as it starts.

443
00:27:02,983 --> 00:27:04,967
Pause to exit and rebuild E.

444
00:27:05,287 --> 00:27:07,977
With all five done,
your group's drawers fill,

445
00:27:07,977 --> 00:27:10,023
and your leaderboard row lights up.

446
00:27:12,167 --> 00:27:14,130
One last question about the key.

447
00:27:14,450 --> 00:27:17,202
It's git-ignored,
so it never reaches GitHub.

448
00:27:17,522 --> 00:27:20,596
But you build the image
and push it to Docker Hub:

449
00:27:20,596 --> 00:27:21,703
is the key inside?

450
00:27:30,550 --> 00:27:34,177
Let's test it,
with an 82 byte stand-in for the real key.

451
00:27:34,497 --> 00:27:39,276
These extra Git commands show it's ignored,
by line 3 of .gitignore.

452
00:27:41,050 --> 00:27:46,213
Now rebuild, and list the secrets folder
inside the image itself, with no mount.

453
00:27:46,533 --> 00:27:50,607
There it is:
mega-pipeline.json, inside the image.

454
00:27:50,927 --> 00:27:54,136
Your real key is in every image
you built today,

455
00:27:54,136 --> 00:27:56,410
because step 2 came before step 5.

456
00:27:56,650 --> 00:27:58,783
Docker doesn't read .gitignore.

457
00:27:59,103 --> 00:28:03,672
Remember lecture 2's parcel:
the build context is the whole folder,

458
00:28:03,672 --> 00:28:07,082
key included,
and the copy line unpacks all of it.

459
00:28:07,402 --> 00:28:10,005
The README's secrets note only covers Git.

460
00:28:10,250 --> 00:28:14,537
The fix, which lecture 2 recommended,
is an extra here:

461
00:28:14,537 --> 00:28:18,591
a file named .dockerignore,
with one line, secrets/.

462
00:28:18,911 --> 00:28:23,733
All five images copied the key,
so pause and add it in all five folders.

463
00:28:25,200 --> 00:28:29,851
Rebuild, and list again:
cannot access, No such file or directory.

464
00:28:30,171 --> 00:28:31,878
The key stayed out of the image.

465
00:28:32,567 --> 00:28:33,925
And the run still works,

466
00:28:33,925 --> 00:28:38,114
because the mount brings your secrets folder
in when the container starts.

467
00:28:38,350 --> 00:28:42,909
The README's optional last section
pushes the image to Docker Hub:

468
00:28:42,909 --> 00:28:46,777
create an access token there,
then log in, tag and push.

469
00:28:47,097 --> 00:28:50,510
Only push an image built
with that .dockerignore.

470
00:28:50,750 --> 00:28:55,186
Log in with just your user name
and paste the token at the prompt;

471
00:28:55,186 --> 00:28:57,875
after -p it stays in your shell history.

472
00:28:58,195 --> 00:29:02,142
If you ever pushed an image with the key,
delete it and tell the staff.

473
00:29:02,384 --> 00:29:04,222
Where the course is heading:

474
00:29:04,222 --> 00:29:08,228
last year's solution kept
the secrets folder outside the repo

475
00:29:08,228 --> 00:29:10,789
and mounted it at /secrets at run time.

476
00:29:11,109 --> 00:29:13,157
No key ever enters the image.

477
00:29:14,500 --> 00:29:15,737
Time to clean up.

478
00:29:16,057 --> 00:29:21,071
In the cloud there's nothing to delete:
you created no machine, bucket or account.

479
00:29:21,317 --> 00:29:24,325
Locally, cd .. back up to the repo.

480
00:29:24,645 --> 00:29:28,079
An extra command lists the five images:

481
00:29:28,079 --> 00:29:35,034
transcribe audio at 1.48 gigabytes,
the other four at about 845 megabytes each.

482
00:29:35,283 --> 00:29:37,011
To keep your work, check Git.

483
00:29:37,331 --> 00:29:41,110
An extra git status shows
your edited cli.py files

484
00:29:41,110 --> 00:29:43,454
and the files in generate text,

485
00:29:43,454 --> 00:29:48,531
but the other four folders hide
their Dockerfiles and project files.

486
00:29:48,767 --> 00:29:50,020
In your own repo,

487
00:29:50,020 --> 00:29:54,149
add those Dockerfiles and project
files with git add -f,

488
00:29:54,149 --> 00:29:58,367
but not the data folders,
and never stop ignoring secrets.

489
00:29:58,617 --> 00:30:03,844
Then pause and remove the five images
with docker image rm, another extra.

490
00:30:04,164 --> 00:30:07,999
Older builds stay behind untagged,
each holding your key,

491
00:30:07,999 --> 00:30:10,691
so docker image prune removes those too.

492
00:30:13,717 --> 00:30:15,722
Five questions to test yourself.

493
00:30:16,042 --> 00:30:20,053
One: which drawer does D,
translate text, read, and who wrote it?

494
00:30:20,373 --> 00:30:23,232
Two:
why doesn't the mount hide the packages,

495
00:30:23,232 --> 00:30:26,218
and what breaks
without UV_PROJECT_ENVIRONMENT?

496
00:30:26,467 --> 00:30:29,489
Three:
after uv add in a throwaway container,

497
00:30:29,489 --> 00:30:32,376
what survives, and what makes it permanent?

498
00:30:32,696 --> 00:30:37,091
Four: which variable points to the key,
and why only from /app?

499
00:30:37,411 --> 00:30:41,101
Five: how can an image hold
a git-ignored key, and the fix?

500
00:30:41,350 --> 00:30:45,787
Answers are in the exercises;
every command is in the study guide.

501
00:30:46,107 --> 00:30:49,281
Next lecture:
your own bucket and service account,

502
00:30:49,281 --> 00:30:50,890
the key outside the repo,

503
00:30:50,890 --> 00:30:54,043
and a script that builds
and runs each container.

